/bin/
/dist/
*.test
.DS_Store

# 🚨 Anchored with a leading slash, and that is not style.
#
# Unanchored, `garrison-agent` matches ANY path component with that name —
# including the source directory cmd/garrison-agent/. Written without the
# slash it silently excluded both command packages from the repo: everything
# built and tested locally, and CI failed with "directory not found" because
# main.go had never been committed at all.
/garrison-agent
/garrison-hub

# Dev-only, and every one of these has shipped to a customer in some repo.
#
# 🚨 `.phpunit.result.cache` in particular: the Flarum skeleton's ignore rule
# misses it, and it was found sitting inside four paid extension zips. It is
# harmless in itself and it is somebody's file listing, in a product they paid
# for, that says nobody checked what went in the box.
/vendor/
/.phpunit.result.cache
/composer.lock

# 🚨 node_modules was tracked until 1.1.0 — 8,396 files, 98% of everything in
# the repository. Harmless while this was private; wrong the moment it is not.
# A public MIT repo whose tree is almost entirely somebody else's dependencies
# is one nobody can read, and every clone pays for it. js/dist stays committed
# on purpose: Flarum loads it and nothing rebuilds it on install, which is why
# CI fails if it has drifted from its source.
js/node_modules/
